1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,387
Dolphin Communication.

2
00:00:02,337 --> 00:00:10,125
Bottlenose dolphins (Tursiops truncatus) possess one of the most sophisticated communication systems in the animal kingdom.

3
00:00:10,125 --> 00:00:19,100
Each individual develops a unique signature whistle during its first year of life — a distinctive frequency-modulated call that functions much like a name.

4
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:28,937
Dolphins use these whistles to broadcast identity, and they copy the whistles of close associates, particularly mothers and calves, to maintain contact.

5
00:00:28,937 --> 00:00:32,975
Echolocation, or biosonar, adds another layer.

6
00:00:32,975 --> 00:00:43,287
A dolphin's melon — a fatty organ in the forehead — focuses clicks into a narrow beam of high-frequency sound, typically between 40 and 130 kHz.

7
00:00:43,287 --> 00:00:53,075
These clicks bounce off objects and return as echoes, which the dolphin perceives through its lower jaw and processes in the brain to construct a three-dimensional acoustic image.

8
00:00:53,075 --> 00:01:02,262
Research at the University of St Andrews has shown that dolphins can discriminate between objects differing by as little as 0.2 millimetres in thickness.

9
00:01:02,262 --> 00:01:08,275
Dolphins live in fluid, fission-fusion societies — groups that frequently split and merge.

10
00:01:08,275 --> 00:01:17,875
A 2021 study in Proceedings of the Royal Society B documented that male dolphins in Shark Bay, Australia, form multi-level alliances.

11
00:01:17,875 --> 00:01:34,625
Individuals cooperate in pairs (first-order alliances), which join into larger second-order alliances of 4 to 14 males, and these may further aggregate into third-order alliances of over 100 individuals — a social complexity previously thought unique to humans.

